issues.fix:
Bound the read in _holdings, keeping round 1's fail-open 'reads as max' rule. Use an explicit gas cap and a 32-byte output buffer, so no returndata is copied beyond one word. Proven in the sandbox (InheritanceVault.sol.fixed next to the sandbox):

    address t = _supportedTokens[i]; uint256 b = type(uint256).max;
    assembly ("memory-safe") { let ptr := mload(0x40) mstore(ptr, shl(224, 0x70a08231)) mstore(add(ptr, 4), address()) let ok := staticcall(100000, t, ptr, 0x24, ptr, 0x20) if and(ok, gt(returndatasize(), 0x1f)) { b := mload(ptr) } }
    held[i] = b;

Results with the patch:
- the full original suite passes: 211/211, including round 1's wrap-and-return and SELFDESTRUCT F09 tests;
- the bomb and burner probes pay at about 226k to 248k gas;
- runtime size falls from 20,992 to 20,845 bytes.

A caller still cannot starve the capped read to mask a return. It would keep less than 1/64 of ~10k gas and could not finish the transaction.

One residual, unchanged from round 1's design: a return INTO a listed token whose reads are failing goes undetected, and becomes sweepable once that token recovers. It needs a listed-token malfunction AND a payee that converts the payout into that very token. Say so in the NatSpec.

Add the bomb, burner and revert cases as a regression test.

