issues.fix:
This is a lead decision, because the plan's F20 text says the cancel 'returns the vault to ACTIVE'. The preferred option is an atomic beneficiaryReplaceRecipient(vaultOwner, vaultId, newRecipient), callable only by the current beneficiary while CLAIM_PENDING, which:
- runs _checkPayee(newRecipient);
- keeps the state CLAIM_PENDING;
- sets claimRecipient;
- restarts claimInitiatedAt, so a full window and the owner's veto time is never shortened;
- re-locks lockedFeeBps as min(old lock, rate in force, recipient-in-force rule), so the fee can only fall;
- emits an event.

This closes (a) and (b), and it also removes round 1's documented abortClaim front-run grief, because abortClaim always finds a claim. The stolen-heir-key cost from round 1 #5 is unchanged either way. Keep or drop the plain cancel. If the design stays as it is, extend the NatSpec and the heir docs. Past the horizon, cancelling lets the owner change the heir before you re-initiate. Before it, anyone holding a chain value, or the owner's automation, can check in and delay you by a full period.

